EXPERIMENT 34 – BOUNDARY VALUE TESTING OF NUMERIC INPUT FIELD USING SELENIUM
AIM
To write a Selenium WebDriver program to test the boundary values of a numeric input field.
ALGORITHM
1. Create a Maven project and add the selenium-java (Selenium 4) dependency to pom.xml.
2. Selenium Manager downloads the browser driver automatically.
3. Create an HTML number field with a valid range of 18 to 60 using JavascriptExecutor on an about:blank page.
4. Identify the boundary values: 17, 18, 19, 59, 60 and 61.
5. For every value:
   - Clear the field.
   - Enter the value using sendKeys().
   - Call the HTML5 checkValidity() method using JavascriptExecutor.
6. Print the validity of every value and verify the results.
7. Close the browser and stop.
CODE
Exercise34.java
import org.openqa.selenium.*;
import org.openqa.selenium.chrome.ChromeDriver;

public class Exercise34 {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        WebDriver driver = new ChromeDriver();

        try {
            driver.manage().window().maximize();

            JavascriptExecutor js = (JavascriptExecutor) driver;
            driver.get("about:blank");

            js.executeScript(
                "document.body.innerHTML = " +
                "\"<input id='age' type='number' min='18' max='60'>\";"
            );

            int[] values = {17, 18, 19, 59, 60, 61};

            for (int v : values) {
                WebElement age = driver.findElement(By.id("age"));

                age.clear();
                age.sendKeys(String.valueOf(v));

                Boolean valid = (Boolean) js.executeScript(
                    "return arguments[0].checkValidity();", age
                );

                System.out.println("Age " + v + " -> valid = " + valid);
            }

        } finally {
            driver.quit();
        }
    }
}


